WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:02.919
I'm planning a trip to this place 
called Soul City.

00:00:03.253 --> 00:00:06.256
It's a town here at the edge of 
North Carolina

00:00:06.548 --> 00:00:10.552
that exists on Google Maps but is missing 
on many others.

00:00:13.972 --> 00:00:15.223
Back in the 70s...

00:00:15.432 --> 00:00:18.435
this was an ambitious and 
experimental town...

00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:20.770
built and led by Black Americans.

00:00:21.521 --> 00:00:26.234
A utopia on thousands of acres 
of former plantation land...

00:00:26.651 --> 00:00:29.446
that would help them gain social 
and economic power.

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:32.073
It was the dream of one man:

00:00:32.407 --> 00:00:35.076
Civil rights activist Floyd McKissick...

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:38.163
who for a time found 
an unexpected ally:

00:00:38.913 --> 00:00:41.082
Republican President Richard Nixon.

00:00:41.916 --> 00:00:43.543
A lot of the town got built.

00:00:43.918 --> 00:00:46.212
A clinic and an industrial park.

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:48.423
People started moving in.

00:00:49.049 --> 00:00:51.968
But the reason you don't see it 
on most maps today... 

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:55.597
is because it doesn't exist 
like it was supposed to.

00:00:56.056 --> 00:00:59.017
If you ride around today, you're going to see 
empty buildings...

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:00.602
vacant buildings...

00:01:01.019 --> 00:01:02.979
particularly that at one time 
they weren't vacant.

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:04.647
We were rising. 
We rose up.

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:06.399
We're just getting there.

00:01:06.608 --> 00:01:08.234
We were just becoming viable.

00:01:08.485 --> 00:01:10.528
That's when the rug got pulled from underneath us.

00:01:11.488 --> 00:01:13.698
So whatever happened to Soul City?

00:01:22.624 --> 00:01:26.044
Floyd B. McKissick was a prolific 
civil rights activist.

00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:30.882
By the 1960s, he was the first Black law student 
at the University of North Carolina.

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:34.469
He was an attorney defending activists 
arrested at protests.

00:01:34.677 --> 00:01:38.264
He led one of the most pivotal civil rights 
organizations in the US.

00:01:38.389 --> 00:01:43.770
And he was also one of the organizers 
of the iconic 1963 March on Washington.

00:01:48.942 --> 00:01:50.985
Here he is next to 
Martin Luther King.

00:01:51.528 --> 00:01:53.988
But McKissick believed progress 
for Black Americans

00:01:53.988 --> 00:01:55.448
had been slow and inadequate.

00:01:55.907 --> 00:01:59.702
So for years, he set his sights on 
one big idea.

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:02.288
What if there was an integrated town.

00:02:02.372 --> 00:02:03.581
Where everyone...

00:02:03.581 --> 00:02:06.543
especially racial minorities and 
the poor can thrive.

00:02:06.543 --> 00:02:07.836
A planned community

00:02:07.836 --> 00:02:10.964
where Black Americans can chart 
a new path towards equality.

00:02:12.132 --> 00:02:13.675
He called it Soul City.

00:02:15.176 --> 00:02:19.472
He was a soldier in World War II
and he'd seen cities getting rebuilt...

00:02:19.806 --> 00:02:20.640
over in Europe.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.685
After they were completely destroyed 
and demolished.

00:02:23.685 --> 00:02:26.813
And he was intrigued and fascinated 
by the way

00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:29.858
you could rebuild cities and 
rebuild communities.

00:02:29.899 --> 00:02:31.109
That's McKissick’s son.

00:02:31.234 --> 00:02:33.736
I'm Floyd B. McKissick Jr.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.239
He's a former North Carolina senator 
and attorney.

00:02:36.531 --> 00:02:39.492
And was director of planning on 
the Soul City Project.

00:02:39.701 --> 00:02:41.452
Oh, I was extremely optimistic.

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:43.204
I mean, I felt this whole city...

00:02:43.538 --> 00:02:46.040
represented new hope, 
new opportunity...

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:49.002
something that could epitomize 
the American dream.

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:51.546
I mean, we lost King. 
We lost Kennedy.

00:02:51.546 --> 00:02:53.173
We lost another Kennedy.
We lost Malcolm X.

00:02:53.256 --> 00:02:55.800
There were so many things happening around us
that there was a reason to say:

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.845
Let's build ourselves an economy 
and live our lives another way.

00:02:59.137 --> 00:03:01.639
That's Jane Ball-Groom, 
a native New Yorker

00:03:01.639 --> 00:03:04.601
who started working with McKissick
in the late 1960s.

00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:07.896
Back when his company 
McKissick Enterprises

00:03:07.896 --> 00:03:10.648
focused on bringing new Black-owned 
businesses to Harlem.

00:03:10.773 --> 00:03:11.941
Are you in this photo?

00:03:11.941 --> 00:03:13.026
This is me.

00:03:13.026 --> 00:03:14.235
It was a time of hope.

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:16.237
The hope was that you could do 
anything you wanted to do.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.331
Unlike groups like the Black Panther Party

00:03:26.331 --> 00:03:29.500
which embraced distinctly socialist values

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:31.044
“All power to the people.”

00:03:31.461 --> 00:03:34.464
McKissick embraced the idea 
of Black capitalism...

00:03:34.631 --> 00:03:36.883
to make his vision of Black power...

00:03:36.966 --> 00:03:37.967
a reality.

00:03:37.967 --> 00:03:40.720
That if capitalism existed 
in our country...

00:03:40.929 --> 00:03:44.098
that African-Americans need to be part
of that economic mainstream.

00:03:44.098 --> 00:03:45.683
They deserved an opportunity

00:03:45.683 --> 00:03:48.144
to share in building their communities.

00:03:48.478 --> 00:03:49.687
How do you get money?

00:03:50.188 --> 00:03:51.231
We're not born with it.

00:03:51.231 --> 00:03:52.649
We can build own businesses.

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:55.693
McKissick’s plan was for Soul City 
to foster industry

00:03:55.693 --> 00:03:58.363
where the money would flow 
from businesses to the community.

00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:01.491
He also had expansive ideas of what 
the community would offer

00:04:01.491 --> 00:04:05.745
like sports facilities, a pool, 
parks, a daycare...

00:04:06.329 --> 00:04:09.832
residential villages built beside churches, 
bike paths...

00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:12.877
a shopping area, housing for 
senior citizens.

00:04:13.294 --> 00:04:17.966
A health clinic known as Health Co Inc 
and Soultech 1:

00:04:18.091 --> 00:04:21.219
A huge 73,000 square foot building

00:04:21.219 --> 00:04:22.595
designed to be an incubator

00:04:22.595 --> 00:04:25.598
for new industry that could bring 
24,000 jobs.

00:04:25.807 --> 00:04:28.768
Your job would be no more than 
10 minutes from your home.

00:04:29.227 --> 00:04:32.021
This would be a place where 
people of all ages

00:04:32.021 --> 00:04:34.274
races and religions 
worked together.

00:04:34.524 --> 00:04:37.652
A city without prejudice, 
without poverty.

00:04:37.986 --> 00:04:39.821
A brand new shining city.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:42.115
By the year 2000

00:04:42.115 --> 00:04:44.325
they projected 
50,000 residents.

00:04:45.076 --> 00:04:47.745
But in order to build this 
new town from scratch

00:04:47.745 --> 00:04:50.623
one that people and businesses 
would want to move to...

00:04:50.999 --> 00:04:53.710
McKissick needed funding and 
political support.

00:04:54.043 --> 00:04:56.546
Luckily, by the late 1960s

00:04:56.546 --> 00:05:00.300
the US government was interested 
in moving people out 

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:03.136
of densely populated areas
and into new towns

00:05:03.136 --> 00:05:04.846
with economic opportunities.

00:05:05.388 --> 00:05:07.807
In 1968, they launched a program

00:05:07.807 --> 00:05:11.602
under the Department of Housing 
and Urban Development or HUD

00:05:11.602 --> 00:05:13.146
which would offer financial assistance

00:05:13.146 --> 00:05:16.733
to a select few proposals for new towns 
across the country.

00:05:17.066 --> 00:05:18.818
McKissick saw it as an opportunity

00:05:18.818 --> 00:05:20.653
to finally make Soul City happen.

00:05:20.903 --> 00:05:24.157
But to get access, he'd still need 
political allies.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:29.495
In 1968, he first pitched his idea 
to President Lyndon Johnson

00:05:29.495 --> 00:05:32.248
but Johnson soon decided not to 
run for reelection.

00:05:32.832 --> 00:05:35.209
So McKissick took his idea 
to the new leading

00:05:35.209 --> 00:05:38.004
Democratic candidate for president, 
Robert F Kennedy.

00:05:38.421 --> 00:05:40.048
Kennedy supported the project...

00:05:40.298 --> 00:05:44.052
but in June of 1968, 
he was assassinated.

00:05:44.594 --> 00:05:47.347
McKissick then made 
a calculated decision...

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.349
one that was incredibly rare

00:05:49.349 --> 00:05:52.018
and controversial for Black activists 
at the time.

00:05:52.477 --> 00:05:55.480
He looked to Republican candidate 
Richard Nixon...

00:05:55.646 --> 00:05:57.690
who was favored to win the next election

00:05:57.857 --> 00:06:00.276
and hoped that he would 
support his project.

00:06:00.568 --> 00:06:01.778
It's about survival.

00:06:02.028 --> 00:06:04.197
And sometimes you have to learn 
how to play the game.

00:06:04.655 --> 00:06:07.116
Later, that year, Nixon won the election.

00:06:07.492 --> 00:06:09.535
And then did something surprising.

00:06:10.328 --> 00:06:14.832
He actually supported McKissick’s dream 
of an anti-racist town.

00:06:15.291 --> 00:06:15.917
But why?

00:06:17.085 --> 00:06:19.462
The Republican Party looked 
a lot different back then.

00:06:19.754 --> 00:06:23.257
It was focused on appealing to the broadest 
possible number of voters.

00:06:23.549 --> 00:06:26.803
It's why we see Nixon's deeply conservative policies,

00:06:26.803 --> 00:06:29.806
including the start to the devastating
war on drugs...

00:06:30.098 --> 00:06:33.309
alongside brief moments of liberal policies.

00:06:33.434 --> 00:06:35.770
“We have passed new laws 
to protect the environment.”

00:06:35.937 --> 00:06:40.566
“Tonight, I therefore propose 
a new family assistance.”

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:44.320
I am also proposing a major expansion 
of daycare centers.

00:06:44.445 --> 00:06:47.657
It wasn't because Nixon necessarily
cared about these policies.

00:06:48.032 --> 00:06:50.618
It was just part of the plan 
to diversify his base.

00:06:50.952 --> 00:06:53.788
And that meant seeking out 
Black activists too.

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:57.875
One of the things that President Nixon 
supported and was intrigued by...

00:06:58.126 --> 00:07:01.754
was this idea of Black capitalism and 
Black entrepreneurship.

00:07:01.838 --> 00:07:04.841
Soul City fit in neatly within that umbrella.

00:07:05.049 --> 00:07:07.635
In 1969, McKissick Enterprises

00:07:07.635 --> 00:07:09.846
publicly announced plans for Soul City.

00:07:09.971 --> 00:07:12.432
“The former head of Core, Floyd McKissick...

00:07:12.765 --> 00:07:16.394
offered an idea today for creation 
of a black built

00:07:16.394 --> 00:07:19.021
black owned town. 

00:07:19.063 --> 00:07:22.233
“In this new town persons will be able to control 
their own destinies...

00:07:22.442 --> 00:07:24.819
McKissick said he anticipates federal aid

00:07:24.819 --> 00:07:26.612
in planning and developing the town.”

00:07:26.946 --> 00:07:31.576
In 1971, Soul City submitted a proposal 
to HUD for funding.

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:34.287
Around that time, McKissick 
officially switched

00:07:34.287 --> 00:07:35.538
to the Republican Party

00:07:35.538 --> 00:07:38.666
and became one of the most prominent 
Black civil rights activists

00:07:38.666 --> 00:07:40.418
supporting Nixon's reelection.

00:07:41.085 --> 00:07:43.129
And by July 1972...

00:07:43.337 --> 00:07:45.465
his alliance with Nixon paid off.

00:07:45.882 --> 00:07:49.469
Soul City was chosen as 1 of only 13 proposals

00:07:49.469 --> 00:07:51.012
for HUD's New Towns program.

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:52.805
McKissick Enterprises would receive

00:07:52.805 --> 00:07:55.433
$14 million in federal funding.

00:07:56.517 --> 00:08:00.480
It was the first development company led 
by Black Americans

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:02.940
to create a new city with government funding.

00:08:03.816 --> 00:08:05.985
That didn't go down well with everyone.

00:08:06.277 --> 00:08:07.570
Soul City had critics.

00:08:07.904 --> 00:08:12.533
One of them was a newly elected North Carolina 
senator named Jesse Helms...

00:08:12.909 --> 00:08:14.744
a deeply conservative Republican...

00:08:14.911 --> 00:08:17.705
and a segregationist with a 
long record against civil rights.

00:08:17.997 --> 00:08:19.415
He was opposed to Soul City

00:08:19.415 --> 00:08:21.334
being a part of the state from the start.

00:08:21.584 --> 00:08:24.462
He couldn't get in the way of federal support 
for the project.

00:08:24.545 --> 00:08:26.422
But he'll come up later in this story.

00:08:28.341 --> 00:08:30.134
McKissick chose here in rural

00:08:30.134 --> 00:08:32.261
Warren County, North Carolina
to build it.

00:08:32.678 --> 00:08:35.556
It was thousands of acres of 
former plantation land

00:08:35.556 --> 00:08:37.808
with almost no existing infrastructure.

00:08:38.059 --> 00:08:40.436
But reclaiming this space was important.

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:43.356
Its history was significant.

00:08:43.397 --> 00:08:45.608
A community that represented the future

00:08:45.608 --> 00:08:48.903
and a land that represented the worst of our past.

00:08:49.237 --> 00:08:52.240
An old former plantation built by slaves.

00:08:52.615 --> 00:08:54.450
We were coming out of what was so dark 

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:57.078
into that light of this—
is possible for us.

00:08:57.078 --> 00:08:59.288
And now we can be somebody 
in this country.

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:03.584
Warren County was also one of many areas 
in the American South

00:09:03.584 --> 00:09:08.548
that had experienced waves of outward migration 
over the past several decades.

00:09:08.548 --> 00:09:12.843
Black people moving from rural 
to urban areas in search of jobs.

00:09:13.177 --> 00:09:16.430
But Soul City was designed to create 
a reverse migration...

00:09:16.681 --> 00:09:18.432
moving people back to the South.

00:09:18.766 --> 00:09:21.143
He said we need to change the face 
of the rural South.

00:09:21.227 --> 00:09:22.562
You need the staff to go south.

00:09:22.687 --> 00:09:24.939
I said, I’ll go, so I talked 
to my husband.

00:09:24.981 --> 00:09:26.774
And I said, well, I'll go 
for two weeks.

00:09:26.857 --> 00:09:29.026
Jane was one of the first people 
to move here...

00:09:29.193 --> 00:09:30.403
and she never left.

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:33.072
Been in Soul City since January 1970.

00:09:33.614 --> 00:09:36.450
We had young engineers 
just getting out of college.

00:09:36.576 --> 00:09:39.370
Architects from all over England...

00:09:39.704 --> 00:09:41.998
Africa, certain places in the United States.

00:09:41.998 --> 00:09:44.625
Soul City was at that time,
literally, a university,

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:45.876
on how to grow a town.

00:09:45.876 --> 00:09:47.086
We were all students.

00:09:47.378 --> 00:09:51.632
By 1973, McKissick Enterprises 
finally broke ground.

00:09:53.259 --> 00:09:54.302
Finally, construction.

00:09:54.302 --> 00:09:56.137
This was what became the Soul City Boulevard.

00:09:57.555 --> 00:09:58.598
We took raw land

00:09:58.598 --> 00:10:01.434
that did not have water and sewer, 
did not have roads

00:10:01.434 --> 00:10:03.894
and we had to really build 
an infrastructure.

00:10:04.145 --> 00:10:07.189
Our first major project was 
a regional water system...

00:10:07.565 --> 00:10:10.359
that not only provided water 
to Soul City...

00:10:10.568 --> 00:10:12.486
but provided water to a region.

00:10:12.528 --> 00:10:14.196
It was the largest regional water system

00:10:14.196 --> 00:10:16.073
built in the state of North Carolina 
at that time.

00:10:16.282 --> 00:10:18.743
It continues to operate and function to this day.

00:10:19.327 --> 00:10:23.122
Being around people who were involved 
with building was transformative.

00:10:23.164 --> 00:10:24.457
Everything was a clay dirt road.

00:10:24.874 --> 00:10:27.877
Seeing a tractor trailer coming down 
on a Monday morning

00:10:27.877 --> 00:10:30.087
hearing buzz saws and smelling paint 

00:10:30.087 --> 00:10:33.633
and seeing old roads transferred 
into cement roads like paved

00:10:33.633 --> 00:10:35.885
driveways was simply amazing.

00:10:36.218 --> 00:10:38.179
So you became taller — I’m five feet. 

00:10:38.554 --> 00:10:40.097
But those days, I was 5’7”.

00:10:40.556 --> 00:10:43.392
I knew I was somebody. To be 
young, gifted and Black.

00:10:43.851 --> 00:10:46.395
I was raised as a Negro child
in Mt. Vernon New York.

00:10:46.854 --> 00:10:50.524
But to be a whole new persona: 
You become something else.

00:10:52.777 --> 00:10:54.820
They also built out electric.

00:10:55.154 --> 00:10:58.908
A fire department, a park, 
and recreational facilities.

00:10:59.325 --> 00:11:00.743
They built out the health clinic...

00:11:01.327 --> 00:11:04.497
along with the massive Soultech 1 
industrial park...

00:11:04.747 --> 00:11:06.040
which would become the new home

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:08.209
of all of McKissick planning operations, too.

00:11:08.834 --> 00:11:12.088
And they built the first of three 
planned residential villages

00:11:12.088 --> 00:11:13.589
where Jane lives to this day.

00:11:14.590 --> 00:11:16.759
But by the late 1970s, Soul City

00:11:16.759 --> 00:11:18.928
still have less than 200 residents

00:11:18.928 --> 00:11:21.180
even though they'd projected thousands by then.

00:11:21.555 --> 00:11:23.224
And they had no real commitments

00:11:23.224 --> 00:11:24.850
from big businesses to move here.

00:11:25.059 --> 00:11:28.229
Even though McKissick was trying to court companies
like General Motors.

00:11:28.813 --> 00:11:32.024
McKissick had a vision, a team, and infrastructure.

00:11:33.067 --> 00:11:34.485
So where were the people?

00:11:35.778 --> 00:11:40.032
“1973 dramatized US dependence 
on foreign oil.”

00:11:40.157 --> 00:11:42.118
“We're in an energy crisis now.”

00:11:42.201 --> 00:11:45.079
“We have at present an absolute shortage 
of natural gas.”

00:11:45.329 --> 00:11:47.748
In the 1970s, industries were hesitant

00:11:47.748 --> 00:11:50.000
to move or invest in new areas.

00:11:50.835 --> 00:11:52.002
This was a problem.

00:11:52.336 --> 00:11:54.088
Soul City was the only new town

00:11:54.088 --> 00:11:56.173
that was trying to build in a rural place

00:11:56.173 --> 00:11:57.800
that was truly freestanding.

00:11:57.883 --> 00:12:00.344
The other projects were coming up next to bigger

00:12:00.344 --> 00:12:02.304
urban areas that could provide jobs.

00:12:02.805 --> 00:12:05.015
There was also the question of its name.

00:12:05.266 --> 00:12:06.892
Even though Soul City repeatedly

00:12:06.892 --> 00:12:09.395
branded itself as a multiracial community.

00:12:09.395 --> 00:12:12.398
Some believed it invoked the idea 
of an all black town.

00:12:12.690 --> 00:12:16.235
If there was a city that was, say, 70% white.

00:12:16.277 --> 00:12:18.362
And about 30% Black

00:12:18.362 --> 00:12:20.698
people would think of that as a 
racially integrated community.

00:12:20.865 --> 00:12:24.326
In Soul City's case, it would have been 70% black...

00:12:24.535 --> 00:12:26.996
and 30% white or of mixed races.

00:12:26.996 --> 00:12:28.998
People would have thought of that 
as an all-Black town.

00:12:30.249 --> 00:12:33.753
Then there was Jesse Helms 
and his attacks on Soul City.

00:12:34.003 --> 00:12:36.505
When have you ever heard of a senator

00:12:36.505 --> 00:12:38.966
fighting to stop the flow of funds...

00:12:39.175 --> 00:12:41.510
into their states or into their districts...

00:12:41.677 --> 00:12:45.222
it's contrary to everything that one 
would logically think about?

00:12:45.514 --> 00:12:47.558
Those are the types of projects 
you would embrace.

00:12:48.184 --> 00:12:50.603
But Jesse Helms was a, 
to be candid

00:12:50.603 --> 00:12:53.439
a segregationist who never repudiated his views.

00:12:53.606 --> 00:12:56.233
His final attack came in 1975...

00:12:56.442 --> 00:12:59.737
when the country was still reeling 
from the aftermath of Watergate.

00:12:59.945 --> 00:13:01.238
“Five people have been arrested

00:13:01.238 --> 00:13:02.865
and charged with breaking into the headquarters

00:13:02.865 --> 00:13:04.325
of the Democratic National Committee.”

00:13:04.325 --> 00:13:07.161
“The biggest White House scandal 
in a century.”

00:13:07.161 --> 00:13:09.997
“The Watergate scandal broke wide open today.”

00:13:10.122 --> 00:13:13.209
“I shall resign the presidency effective 
at noon tomorrow.”

00:13:13.542 --> 00:13:15.336
Helms capitalized on the scandal

00:13:15.336 --> 00:13:18.130
and the fact that McKissick and Soul City 
were tied to Nixon.

00:13:18.172 --> 00:13:20.966
He accused Soul City of various 
financial wrongdoings...

00:13:21.133 --> 00:13:23.969
repeatedly calling it a waste 
and a boondoggle.

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:27.097
And Helms, along with another 
North Carolina congressman

00:13:27.097 --> 00:13:29.475
demanded a federal audit on the project.

00:13:29.767 --> 00:13:32.520
That investigation froze HUD funds

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:34.480
and progress for over a year.

00:13:34.897 --> 00:13:38.317
They had kind of a chilling impact 
on Soul City's reputation.

00:13:38.776 --> 00:13:41.320
The Raleigh News and Observer 
wrote a series of articles

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:43.697
on alleged mismanagement of funds at Soul City.

00:13:43.739 --> 00:13:46.700
They ran editorials, including 
cartoons of McKissick...

00:13:46.826 --> 00:13:48.869
that slowly destroyed public opinion.

00:13:48.911 --> 00:13:50.621
They could not conceive

00:13:50.621 --> 00:13:53.707
of an African American development company 
going out there

00:13:53.707 --> 00:13:57.753
and having the expertise and capacity 
to build a new town.

00:13:58.003 --> 00:13:59.296
They thought it was fraud.

00:13:59.338 --> 00:14:01.006
They thought there was abuse

00:14:01.006 --> 00:14:02.925
and all types of allegations were made.

00:14:03.551 --> 00:14:05.636
By December 1975

00:14:05.678 --> 00:14:09.640
A government audit cleared Soul City 
of any major wrongdoing,

00:14:10.391 --> 00:14:11.934
but it was too late.

00:14:13.269 --> 00:14:14.353
I went to lunch one day.

00:14:14.353 --> 00:14:17.147
I was coming back into Soultech 1, 
into the parking lot

00:14:17.147 --> 00:14:20.192
and one of the workers stopped me, says Jane,
it’s over.

00:14:20.192 --> 00:14:21.277
I said what do you mean it’s over?

00:14:21.485 --> 00:14:23.028
He said they've shut the project down.

00:14:23.779 --> 00:14:27.032
The bad press had scared people 
and big business away.

00:14:27.157 --> 00:14:30.077
And in 1979, HUD pulled the plug.

00:14:30.452 --> 00:14:32.454
It would no longer fund a Soul city.

00:14:32.663 --> 00:14:35.165
“Today, the Department of Housing 
and Urban Development...

00:14:35.416 --> 00:14:38.752
said it's cutting its ties to Soul City, North Carolina.”

00:14:38.794 --> 00:14:40.838
“There have been hopes that industry would come here.”

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:42.089
“That never happened.”

00:14:42.131 --> 00:14:43.465
“That's why the government concluded

00:14:43.465 --> 00:14:44.967
the project would never make money.”

00:14:45.009 --> 00:14:46.719
“I think racial prejudice is

00:14:46.719 --> 00:14:48.846
what makes people say we have failed.”

00:14:48.846 --> 00:14:49.763
“I said what are we gonna do?”

00:14:49.805 --> 00:14:51.223
“I'm too old to move in a place else.”

00:14:51.265 --> 00:14:52.600
“You know and I wouldn't like to move.”

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:53.767
“We live at home.”

00:14:53.767 --> 00:14:56.145
“Without federal money
and without industry here...

00:14:56.437 --> 00:14:58.105
This town will not survive.”

00:14:58.731 --> 00:15:00.858
McKissick couldn't fund the project on his own.

00:15:01.150 --> 00:15:03.986
He had to foreclose and sell off most of the land.

00:15:07.948 --> 00:15:11.285
Today, you can still see a few remnants of what once was.

00:15:12.244 --> 00:15:14.872
There's the church and the old 
Health Co building.

00:15:15.789 --> 00:15:17.666
The clinic operated in the community

00:15:17.666 --> 00:15:19.919
for decades after Soul City shut down.

00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:23.130
But it's been closed for years and is now abandoned

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:24.673
and overgrown with plants.

00:15:25.633 --> 00:15:27.801
And then there’s Soultech 1.

00:15:28.135 --> 00:15:31.221
Today, it's been taken over 
by a new industry.

00:15:31.722 --> 00:15:33.349
A prison work complex.

00:15:33.933 --> 00:15:36.518
It does not symbolically incorporate

00:15:36.518 --> 00:15:38.896
what my father would have thought of as providing...

00:15:39.104 --> 00:15:40.731
a second chance for people.

00:15:41.398 --> 00:15:43.442
Soul City represented a second chance.

00:15:44.026 --> 00:15:46.779
The remains of Soul City are a testament to what

00:15:46.779 --> 00:15:50.824
they were able to accomplish with
little funding and short time.

00:15:50.824 --> 00:15:51.909
Within a seven year period of time

00:15:51.909 --> 00:15:55.579
we built the roads, the houses, complexes...

00:15:55.746 --> 00:15:57.373
from nothing came... a lot.

00:15:58.248 --> 00:15:59.208
We rose up.

00:15:59.208 --> 00:16:01.001
Red clay to what you see today.

00:16:01.418 --> 00:16:05.422
The park pool and sports facility 
still serve the community

00:16:05.422 --> 00:16:07.925
as well as the roads and water infrastructure.

00:16:08.217 --> 00:16:10.511
Many of the homes along cul de sacs remain,

00:16:10.761 --> 00:16:13.180
though just a handful of the original Soul City

00:16:13.180 --> 00:16:14.473
residents still live there.

00:16:14.682 --> 00:16:15.891
I didn't want to go back.

00:16:16.016 --> 00:16:17.643
I said, I'm not going anywhere. 
I'm staying right here.

00:16:19.269 --> 00:16:22.147
Floyd McKissick also never gave up on this place.

00:16:22.398 --> 00:16:25.150
He lived here until his passing in 1991...

00:16:25.484 --> 00:16:28.112
and was laid to rest in Soul City 
near the home

00:16:28.112 --> 00:16:29.321
he and his family built.

00:16:29.697 --> 00:16:33.117
Though his dream was cut short 
to his family and friends...

00:16:33.367 --> 00:16:36.495
this will always be a place 
that embodies possibility...

00:16:36.912 --> 00:16:37.830
not failure.

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:39.039
That's what Soul City is.

00:16:39.665 --> 00:16:40.874
It's about hope.

00:16:41.083 --> 00:16:42.292
It's about dreams.

00:16:42.292 --> 00:16:45.254
Did it become the reality that we envisioned?

00:16:45.629 --> 00:16:46.839
The answer is no.

00:16:47.798 --> 00:16:49.091
But can we learn from that?

00:16:49.508 --> 00:16:52.052
And can we think about what that symbolizes

00:16:52.052 --> 00:16:53.804
in terms of moving forward in America?

00:16:54.388 --> 00:16:56.140
The answer is absolutely yes.

00:16:59.435 --> 00:17:00.269
Thanks for watching.

00:17:00.269 --> 00:17:02.312
I'm Ranjani, the producer 
of this episode.

00:17:02.354 --> 00:17:03.772
So one of the interesting things

00:17:03.772 --> 00:17:07.151
I learned while researching this history
is that Warren County

00:17:07.151 --> 00:17:11.572
where Soul City is also, made national news 
again in 1982

00:17:11.572 --> 00:17:14.742
as the birthplace of the environmental 
justice movement.

00:17:14.825 --> 00:17:17.619
It's where Black residents led an iconic protest...

00:17:17.745 --> 00:17:18.912
against the state's plan

00:17:18.912 --> 00:17:21.665
to place a toxic landfill in their community

00:17:21.665 --> 00:17:24.918
and sparked a national movement 
against environmental racism

00:17:24.918 --> 00:17:26.128
that's still going today.

00:17:26.336 --> 00:17:29.715
Floyd McKissick was one of hundreds 
of protesters arrested.

00:17:30.007 --> 00:17:33.427
I'll put a few links about that history 
in the description below, if you're interested.

00:17:33.552 --> 00:17:35.387
And before you go, I have a request.

00:17:35.512 --> 00:17:37.806
We pick stories like this for Missing Chapter

00:17:37.806 --> 00:17:41.226
to help as many people as possible
have access to hidden history

00:17:41.226 --> 00:17:42.311
without a paywall

00:17:42.311 --> 00:17:45.230
to help keep our journalism free and 
support more of our work.

00:17:45.272 --> 00:17:48.942
You can go to vox.com/give 
to make a contribution.

00:17:49.109 --> 00:17:52.446
We're gearing up for a big year full of 
ambitious projects

00:17:52.446 --> 00:17:54.948
and are looking to hit 85,000 total

00:17:54.948 --> 00:17:57.284
contributions by the end of 2023.

00:17:57.451 --> 00:17:59.536
So if you can contribute at the link below

00:17:59.536 --> 00:18:00.746
to help us reach that goal.

00:18:00.913 --> 00:18:02.748
Thanks again for watching and supporting.

